---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GliderBallast from '../../components/GliderBallast.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Glider ballast calculator"
  description="Ballast an underwater glider against TEOS-10 in-situ density: how much lead to add, what it should read in the tank, and whether the buoyancy engine can cover the water it will fly in."
>
  <div class="container">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/data/">Data &amp; Tools</a></p>
    <h1>Glider ballast calculator</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      A glider flies by being slightly the wrong weight on purpose. Ballasting
      sets the fixed part of that so the buoyancy engine's own travel straddles
      neutral across the water the vehicle will actually fly in — and the
      densities come from the same
      full-precision <a href="/data/seawater/">TEOS-10 engine</a> as the
      seawater calculator, not from a table.
    </p>

    <GliderBallast />

    <section class="about">
      <h2>How to use it</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Pick your vehicle, then <strong>replace every number in the glider
          panel</strong> from your own ballast sheet or tank test. The ones
          that ship are stand-ins.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enter the tank water. If you have floated the glider and read a
          scale, put that in <em>Measured buoyancy</em> — that reading is a
          better measure of the vehicle's mass than any figure on paper, and
          the mass box will follow it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enter the three water points from a cast, a climatology or a
          forecast: the surface, the mixed layer, and the deepest inflection
          the mission will use.
        </li>
        <li>
          Choose which one to be neutral at, and read the ballast change.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What it is doing</h2>

      <h3>One equation</h3>
      <p>
        Buoyancy is the weight of the water displaced less the weight of the
        vehicle: <code>B = ρ(S<sub>A</sub>, t, p) · V(t, p) − m</code>. The
        density is TEOS-10's, in situ — at the actual pressure and temperature,
        not at the surface. The displaced volume is your hull's, moved by its
        compressibility and its thermal expansion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ballasting for a point means setting <code>m</code> so
        that <code>B</code> is zero there. Everything else on the page follows
        from that one line.
      </p>

      <h3>Which way the hull moves against the water</h3>
      <p>
        This is the part most often had backwards, and it is not a fact about
        gliders — it is a comparison between two numbers. If your hull
        compresses <em>less</em> per dbar than seawater does, the glider gains
        buoyancy on the way down; if more, it loses it. Seawater is about
        4.3×10<sup>-6</sup> per dbar. The page states the answer for your
        vehicle in grams per 100 dbar rather than leaving it to a rule of
        thumb, and does the same for a five-degree change in temperature.
      </p>

      <h3>Surfacing and diving</h3>
      <p>
        Reaching neutral is not the question that decides a mission. The
        engine has to have enough travel to be <em>positive</em> at the
        shallowest point and <em>negative</em> at the deepest one, with the
        pump at its stops. Those two margins are computed at the shallowest
        and deepest of the three points you entered — not at whichever you
        listed first — and a vehicle that cannot do both gets a warning rather
        than a ballast figure that flies nothing.
      </p>

      <h3>Where it stops</h3>
      <p>
        The hull model is first order in pressure and in temperature, which is
        all a ballast sheet's two coefficients can support. It does not know
        about oil volume moving with temperature, air trapped in the fairing,
        fresh-water absorption by foam, or the vehicle's attitude. It assumes
        the three water points describe the water the glider will meet, which
        is a forecast and not a measurement. Treat the answer as the starting
        point for a tank test, not a substitute for one.
      </p>

      <h3>Credits</h3>
      <p>
        Densities from TEOS-10 — see
        the <a href="/data/seawater/">seawater calculator</a> for what that means
        and how it is checked. The ballast arithmetic is ours, and so is any
        error in it; there is no published standard for this the way there is
        for the equation of state, so it is held to identities, to TEOS-10's
        own compressibility, and to sums anybody can redo on paper.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin-block: var(--space-xl) 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-block: var(--space-xs) var(--space-md);
  }

  .intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .about {
    margin-block: var(--space-2xl);
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
  }

  .about h2 {
    margin-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-md);
  }

  .about h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .about p,
  .about li {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .about ol {
    padding-inline-start: 1.3em;
  }

  .about code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
